Information on building a LispView module and on building a LispView
image (a Lisp image that contains LispView):
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

To build a LispView image from the material in this LispView 1.1 source
release (and an existing Lisp image) you can first build a LispView module
and then disksave an image containing that module.  We recommend that these
two stages be done in separate Lisp sessions; some details are given below.
The remarks below indicate how builds have been done in the environment
at Sun.  You may need to customize the build process and release files
to meet your own requirements.

PRELIMINARIES

The directory in which this source release is installed will be
called the top-level directory (of the LispView source release), or 
<lv-top-level-dir>.  The suggested build process and the build files
included with the release assume that <lv-top-level-dir> contains:
src   --  subdirectory with LispView-specific source
xview --  subdirectory with source for a generic foreign interface to
	  XView, and OpenWindows Version 2.0 XView libraries
lispview -- symbolic link to "." (i.e., to <lv-top-level-dir>)
build.lisp --  top-level build file for creating a LispView module

["src" and "xview" contain other build.lisp files used by the top-level
build.lisp].  There are other components in the release, but the four
listed here are the ones needed for builds.
[Relative pathnames in the text below are relative to <lv-top-level-dir>
unless otherwise indicated.]

The instructions and files assume you are using Sun Common Lisp 4.0
(patch level 4.0.2) or Lucid Common Lisp for Sun, with CLOS, on a
SPARCsystem (or Sun-4) computer.  The build uses the OpenWindows 2.0
libraries supplied with the release.  The resulting LispView images
have been run successfully with the OpenWindows 3.0 server as well as
the OpenWindows 2.0 server.
 
[This release of LispView has not been tested with Lisp 4.0.1, although it
may work with Lisp 4.0.1.]

[If you will be recompiling foreign code, see the remarks on OPENWINHOME
in the file xview/NOTE.Makefile]


CREATING A NEW LISPVIEW MODULE

Loading the top-level  build.lisp  file recompiles and loads LispView and
creates one binary file  lispview.sbin  (the LispView module) that contains
all of the compiled LispView files.  The top-level build.lisp file assumes
that the current directory is <lv-top-level-dir>.

The procedure in more detail:

Make sure you have write permission in <lv-top-level-dir> and in its
"src" and "xview" subdirectories (adjust permissions temporarily if
necessary).  If there is already a lispview.sbin in <lv-top-level-dir>,
you may want to save it under another name to keep it from being deleted
by the build (at least until you have tested the new module).
[In fact a lispview.sbin built with this source release is included with
the release, in <lv-top-level-dir>.]
The size of the module is about 2 MB; and another 2 MB of disk space will
be needed temporarily for files produced during the module build and 
automatically deleted at the end of the build.

Start up a Lisp 4.0.2 image that includes CLOS and the compiler.
[For our builds at Sun, this image has been created from the Lisp 4.0 FCS
lisp-4-0-base image, with CLOS module and Lisp patches from the Lisp 4.0.2
Patch Set, and nothing else.
We usually start it up with the "-n" option to avoid loading any lisp-init
file, even though there is no disksave in this stage of the build.]

Work in the top-level directory:
(cd "<lv-top-level-dir>")

Use the production compiler; for example, set up your compilation
environment with:
(proclaim '(optimize (speed 3) (safety 1) (compilation-speed 0)))

Load the top-level build file:
(load "build.lisp")

This will compile and load all the appropriate files (from "xview"
and "src") and create lispview.sbin in <lv-top-level-dir>.
[Actually the extension of the LispView module binary is obtained from
LCL:*load-binary-pathname-types*; the default is "sbin".  The 
intermediate files produced are removed by build.lisp; the build process
may take approximately 45 minutes.]
[The version of the LispView module produced by the source release as
shipped is 1.1.20, as indicated by the variable lv:*lispview-version*]

Quit Lisp.  (Restore the original directory permissions as needed.)


[Note: If you set up SCCS directories for the LispView sources, note
that the build.lisp files in "xview" and "src" include code that
checks for "SCCS" in those subdirectories; if "SCCS" is present and it
contains the appropriate history files, then the build uses it to get
the most recent versions before compiling.  See those build.lisp files
for details.]


BUILDING A LISPVIEW IMAGE WITH A NEW LISPVIEW MODULE

This stage should be done in a fresh Lisp session [to be free of
garbage from the compilation step above].

Basically, a LispView image is obtained by loading lispview.sbin
into a Lisp image that has CLOS, and then disksaving.  The two alternative
procedures suggested below include some CLOS precompilation and memory
management.

Procedure 1:

Check that you have write permission and at least 14.3 MB of available
space in the directory where you want to put the image.
[An image built on the Lisp 4.0 Application Environment would be smaller,
about 12.3 MB.]

Start up a Lisp 4.0.2 image that includes CLOS.
[This image normally would have been created from the Lisp 4.0 FCS
lisp-4-0-base or lisp-4-0-app image, with CLOS module and Lisp patches
from the Lisp 4.0.2 Patch Set, and nothing else.  The image should not
already contain a window system.
Since a disksave will be done, probably you want to start up the image
with the "-n" option to avoid loading a lisp-init file.]

In images with the compiler do some memory management:
#+:COMPILER (LCL:change-memory-management :growth-limit 1500)

Load the LispView module created above:
(load "<lv-top-level-dir>/lispview.sbin")

[The module should be left in <lv-top-level-dir> since it will look for
foreign code in "lispview/xview".  Otherwise you would need to set up
appropriate links.]
[Loading lispview.sbin will generate various warnings about redefined
functions and undefined foreign symbols; these can be ignored.]

Do CLOS precompilation if the compiler is present:
#+:COMPILER (locally (declare (optimize (speed 3) (safety 1) (compilation-speed 0)))
	(CLOS:precompile-generic-functions))

We usually discard source code recording info:
(LCL:discard-source-file-info)

Disksave:
(LCL:disksave "<pathname-for-your-new-image>" :full-gc t :verbose t)

Quit Lisp.  [End of Procedure 1]


[Note: The steps above assume that a Lisp+CLOS image has been created
earlier.  If, instead, CLOS and the patches are loaded in the same
session as lispview.sbin, then a "GC relocation" error might occur
during the disksave (this has been seen intermittently).  You should
be able to avoid this problem by doing a reserved memory expansion
beforehand; specifically, in place of the above memory management
command, use:
#+:COMPILER (LCL:change-memory-management :growth-limit 1500 :expand-reserved 25)
]


Procedure 2:

Alternatively you can "plug" the new LispView module into the standard
user build for a Lisp 4.0.2 image.  The build-lisp2 script that comes
with the Lisp 4.0.2 Patch Set does essentially the same steps as
Procedure 1.  [Distinguish that build-lisp2 shell script from the
LispView source release build.lisp file.]

To do this, use a symbolic link to the new module, rather than moving
files around.  Using the link insures that the module will pick up foreign
code from your source release "lispview/xview" subdirectory, rather than
from your earlier release directory.  [The only difference between the
foreign code in the current source release and the foreign code in the
Lisp 4.0.2 Patch Set is that the current source release has two new
patches.  One of these, ol_pixmap_label.o, is automatically loaded by the
LispView module; the other, p_list.o, is a patch you may choose to load
manually (see the release-notes file in the "doc" subdirectory).]

For example, if the Lisp+CLOS image used to create lispview.sbin above
was built with the Lisp 4.0.2 Patch Set, you could do the following
[this assumes the image does not already contain a window system]:

% cd <your-Lisp-4.0.2-Patch-Set-directory>

Save the lispview.sbin that came with that patch set:
% mv lispview.sbin lispview.sbin.ORIG

Make a link to the new module:
% ln -s <lv-top-level-dir>/lispview.sbin lispview.sbin

Invoke build-lisp2 and specify the Lisp+CLOS image used above as your
starting image.  Follow the prompts.

Note: build-lisp2 will say it wants to load CLOS when you select LispView;
let it go ahead, it will not actually do that load when it finds CLOS
already present.

Note: build-lisp2 does not have the production compiler wrapper around
(CLOS:precompile-generic-functions) [see Procedure 1], so Procedure 1
might generate more efficient code.

End of Procedure 2

[Note: If you try Procedure 2 with the Lisp 4.0 FCS build-lisp script
(instead of build-lisp2) AND load CLOS and patches in the same session
as lispview.sbin, then a "GC relocation" error might occur during the
disksave.  In that case, use Procedure 1 and see the Note at the end of
Procedure 1.]
